  "title": "Problems",
  "body": " Problems    A nucleus with mass in its ground state and initially at rest absorbs a photon of energy . After absorbing the photon, the nucleus is raised to an excited state, with mass , and unknown momentum .  Draw a picture of this interaction.  Write down the two conservation laws in terms of , , and given numerical values.  Solve for and .        Calculate the speed of the recoiling excited-state nucleus in problem . Compare with the case of photon emission, done as example in the text. Are the recoil velocities the same for emission and absorption?      A particle of mass and energy approaches a stationary particle of mass . The particles collide and form a single particle of mass . Determine by using the conservation laws.      An incident proton, mass , strikes a target proton at rest with just enough energy to create an electron-positron pair. (The two protons are still present after the collision.) A positron is the antiparticle of an electron; both the electron and positron have masses . Calculate the minimum energy needed by the incident proton in the frame where the target proton is initially at rest. (Hint: After the collision, both protons and the electron-positron pair all move together with the same velocity.)      A particle of mass and momentum hits and sticks to a particle of mass , initially at rest.  Find the mass of the composite particle and its velocity.  How much kinetic energy is converted to matter?        A deuteron (mass ) absorbs a photon and splits into a proton (mass ) and a neutron (mass ). What is the minimum energy of the photon required to do this?      The easiest and most immediately promising nuclear reaction to be used for fusion power is the fusion of a deuterium ( H) nucleus, with mass , and a tritium ( H) nucleus, with mass . The fusion reaction produces a He nucleus of mass , and a free neutron of mass :   Is rest energy converted to kinetic energy or vice-versa? Support your answer.  Calculate the amount of energy that is converted.        In a fission process, a slow neutron causes a uranium nucleus ( ) to split into a barium nucleus (mass  ) and a krypton nucleus ( ), plus two excess neutrons (actually 3 including the original neutron, but that is present before the process as well), each of mass . Calculate the energy converted from rest energy to kinetic energy in this process.      A photon of momentum traveling along the positive -axis strikes a particle of mass , which is initially at rest. The result of the collision is simply two photons: photon travels backward, along the negative -axis and photon travels forward, along the positive -axis.  Draw before and after pictures of the interaction.  Find the energies of and after the collision.        Based on the plot in Figure , answer the following questions:  Why is a fusion reaction a more efficient power source ( pound for pound ) than a fission reaction?  A supermassive star goes supernova after it has run out of hydrogen to fuse, at which point it starts fusing helium into heavier elements, then fusing those into heavier elements, etc., until it gets to iron (Fe). Up until this point, the fusion reactions produce kinetic energy and heat, maintaining the star. But after the star has fused its materials into iron, it stops producing kinetic energy, collapses very suddenly and goes Blammo!! (This is a supernova.) What is so special about iron, and why can't the star produce additional kinetic energy after this point?        A flower absorbs a (higher energy) photon of ultraviolet light and emits a (lower energy) red photon. Describe what happens to the mass of the flower first when it absorbs the ultraviolet photon and then later when it emits the red photon. Would you expect any mass changes during this process to be noticeable? Explain why or why not.      It's not just nuclear reactions that involving converting mass-energy to kinetic energy. Chemical reactions, such as combustion, also do this, although the effect on the masses is hardly noticeable. For instance, when a car burns one gallon of gasoline, of kinetic energy is released. Consider the total mass of the reactants (i.e., all the molecules of the gasoline and oxygen before the reaction) versus the total mass of all the molecules in the chemical products after the reaction. How much mass (in kg) is lost in this reaction (i.e., total mass of reactants minus total mass of products)?    "
