  "body": " Sketching $p$ -$V$ diagrams   A gas in a cylinder starts at a temperature , volume , and pressure . It is expanded at constant pressure to twice its initial volume. It is then compressed isothermally back to the original volume, after which it is cooled down at constant volume to a temperature which is half of the initial temperature. Sketch a - diagram for the entire process .   Solution:    Solution for Example . Constant-pressure expansion (1 2) to twice the initial volume, followed by isothermal compression (2 3) back to initial volume, followed by cooling at constant volume (3 4) to temperature half of the initial value.    The - diagram for the process is displayed in Figure . The illustrated constant-pressure expansion also results in a temperature increase. Since , a doubling of the volume (to at constant-pressure results in a doubling of the temperature (to ) as well. So, after the isothermal compression , the gas is at twice its initial temperature and therefore twice the initial pressure; i.e., . (You could also easily determine the pressure by noting that with and both constant, .) The final process brings the gas to a temperature half of its initial value, and since the final volume is the same as the initial volume, the final pressure must by half of the initial pressure; i.e., .   "
