  "body": " Examples   Calculating work for gas processes   Calculate the work done on the gas for each of the three processes in Example (see Figure ).   Solution: Let's deal with each of the three processes in turn:  Process : This is a constant-pressure expansion, so can be taken out of the integral for work: The value of is positive, which makes sense because the gas is expanding (the volume is increasing). A short-cut to the result would be to recognize that is the area under the curve, which in this case is a rectangle: The work done on the gas is J.  Process : This is an isothermal compression, so . We don't know how many moles of gas there are, but we could figure it out using the Ideal Gas Law. But we aren't going to do that , because we can replace the entire product with either or . So, Note that this is a positive number, which makes sense since it is a compression, and we know that is positive for a compression. Note also that the work for the process has a larger magnitude than that for process , which also makes sense because there is a larger area under the curve than under the curve .  Process : This is a constant-volume process, so . This is also consistent with work as area under the curve, as the area under the curve for the process is zero.       Adiabatic compression   A diatomic gas in a well-insulated container has an initial pressure, temperature, and volume of , , and . The gas is compressed to a volume of . Calculate the pressure and temperature of the gas after the compression.   Solution: Since the container is insulated, , and this is an adiabatic process. And since the gas is composed of diatomic molecules at a temperature that isn't too high, there are 5 degrees of freedom for the gas and . Since this is an adiabatic process, , so so , which implies   To determine the temperature after the compression, remember that in any adiabatic process , so which implies that   Note that the gas heats up, which is characteristic of any adiabatic compression.     A complete cycle   Consider a fixed amount of an ideal gas of diatomic molecules undergoing the processes shown in Figure . The gas starts at point A in the diagram and expands along the path with no heat flowing into or out of the gas.  (a) Calculate the pressure of the gas at point B after the expansion. (b) Determine the net work done on the gas and the heat flow in one complete cycle ( ).   For Example .     Solution: (a) Since the process is adiabatic, and so   (b) We will calculate the work done on the gas for each of the three processes and add up the results to get the net work. To figure out the total heat flow we could do something similar i.e., calculate for each process and then add them up. But there is an easier way: since we know that for the complete cycle, once we have determined the total for the complete cycle, we can use the First Law of Thermodynamics to get for the complete cycle.  Let's start with process . This is an adiabatic process, so . Calculating work is tricky for adiabatic processes, so we'll find first and then use the First Law to get . This is a diatomic gas, so and . can be found with the pressures and volumes given (see ):   We can now find the work done on the gas via the First Law:   Process is a constant-volume process (isochoric), so .  Process is a constant-pressure process (isobaric), so   So, the net work done on the gas for the complete cycle is   As for the total heat flow for the entire cycle, we can use the First Law:   This means the net heat flow during the entire cycle is flowing out of the gas.    "
