  "title": "Problems",
  "body": " Problems    Three identical gas-cylinder systems are compressed from the same initial state to final states that have the same volume, one isothermally, one adiabatically, and one isobarically. Which system has the most work done on it? The least?      The relation between thermal energy and temperature depends on the type of gas molecule: for a monatomic gas, and for a diatomic gas, . In your own words and discussing explicitly the microscopic picture of gases explain why it is that you need to put more thermal energy into a diatomic gas than for a monatomic gas to raise the temperature by .      Consider a piston in a car engine that is compressing an air-gasoline mixture before ignition, all in the gas state.  Under what circumstances would the compression be adiabatic? (There are a couple of ways to get an adiabatic compression, one of which is more relevant to a car engine.)  Use the First Law of Thermodynamics to argue that the gas temperature increases if the compression is adiabatic. Where does that additional thermal energy come from during this process?        A gas with moles of molecules starts with a temperature , pressure and volume . Draw a - diagram for the following multi-step processes (and label each process with its letter a, b, or c):  The gas expands at a constant pressure to twice its initial volume.  The gas is then compressed isothermally back to its initial volume.  The gas then expands adiabatically back to its initial pressure.        A gas with moles of molecules starts with a temperature , pressure and volume . Draw a - diagram for the following multi-step processes:  The gas is heated up at constant volume, doubling its initial temperature.  The gas expands adiabatically until the pressure returns to .  The container no longer expands but a vent is opened, allowing gas to escape until the pressure returns to the original pressure .  The gas is compressed isothermally until the volume returns to the original volume .        Calculate the work done on a gas as it undergoes the process shown in the - diagram ( Figure ).   For Prob. .        A piston initially contains of a monatomic gas at a pressure of , and a volume of . The gas expands at constant pressure to a volume of . Calculate the work done on the gas during this process.      A piston initially contains of a diatomic gas at a pressure of , a temperature and a volume of . The gas is ignited, raising the temperature rapidly to without a significant change in the volume. Calculate the work done on the gas during this process.      A monatomic ideal gas undergoes an isothermal compression from an initial volume of and pressure to a final volume and pressure of and , respectively. The temperature along the isotherm is .  Determine the number of moles of this gas.  Determine the work done on the gas during this process.  Determine the heat flowing into the gas during this process.        Consider a fixed amount of an ideal monatomic gas undergoing the adiabatic process illustrated in Figure .   For problem .     Calculate the number of moles in the gas.  Calculate the work done on the gas between points A and B .        Use the Equipartition Theorem to determine a relation between the thermal energy change and the temperature change for a monatomic flattium gas where the molecules can move only in two dimensions.  Do the same thing, but this time for a diatomic flattium gas.       In problem you explained the different factors, vs. , that appear in thermal energy expressions for diatomic and monatomic gases. The expressions differ because of the additional rotational degrees of freedom for the diatomic molecules, which adds terms to the energy used in the equipartition theorem. But if the gas is really hot, it is also possible to excite vibrations in the diatomic molecules, resulting in two more terms in the equation for the energy of a diatomic molecule: one for the kinetic energy of the vibration and one for the potential energy of the vibrating molecule. What would you expect for the relation between and for such a hot diatomic gas?      A piston contains 0.65 moles of diatomic nitrogen gas (N ), with an initial pressure and volume of and , respectively. The piston compresses the gas adiabatically to a volume of . Calculate the pressure of the gas after this compression.      A piston contains of monatomic helium gas, with an initial volume and temperature of and , respectively. The piston expands the gas adiabatically to a volume of . Calculate the temperature of the gas after this expansion.      A fixed amount of diatomic ideal gas goes through the cycle as shown in Figure .   For problem .     : adiabatic compression   : constant-pressure expansion   : cooling at constant volume  Fill in the chart below for the change in thermal energy, heat flow in, and work done on the gas for each of the processes and for the complete cycle. Show all work.       For problem .       of diatomic ideal gas undergoes the processes shown in the - diagram of Figure . The table below has boxes for the change in thermal energy of the gas, the heat added to the gas, and the work done on the gas, all in joules. Energy values have already been entered in two of the boxes. Fill in the remaining entries with values accurate to three significant digits. Be sure to show your work for each entry.         The - diagram in Figure shows a cyclic process for 2 moles of an ideal monatomic gas.   For problem .     Determine the temperature of the gas at state A .  Determine the heat flow into the gas during the process A B .  Determine the heat flow into the gas during the process B C .  Is the work done on the system for the complete cycle positive, negative or zero? (No calculation required.)     "
