  "body": "  Entropy applies to more than just heat flow. We can use entropy and the second law of thermodynamics to discuss movement of air in a room.  Consider a room with only 100 gas molecules. Theoretically, the gas molecules can move anywhere in the room. Calculate the probability that all 100 of the molecules will be found on one particular side of the room.  Now, consider a real room with a realistic amount of gas in it let's say that there are gas molecules in the room. Calculate the probability that all of these gas molecules will be found in one particular side of the room. (Note: the probability is so small that your calculator or computer might simply give 0 for the answer.)  Is it reasonable to say that you will never find all the air in one side of the room?  Now, write a couple of sentences explaining why it is (from a probability perspective) that when a perfume bottle is opened, the scent of the perfume will spread throughout the room.  After the perfume smell has spread throughout the room, would you expect all of the perfume molecules to go back into the bottle? Discuss this using the entropic form of the second law of thermodynamics.     "
