  "body": " Automobile Efficiency   The internal combustion engine of an automobile is a heat engine. Yes, there is gas being consumed, but that's being burned to provide the high temperature of the hot reservoir. From there on, the engine functions as a heat engine.  The hot reservoir is about and the cold reservoir is almost air temperature, but typically more like . Burning a gallon of gasoline provides about of heat. What is the upper limit on how much work can be extracted from a gallon of gas, and how much heat must be dumped?   Solution: The moment you see the words upper limit (or similar language), then you can pull out the second law of thermodynamics: . For this problem, , since the reservoirs are the only parts of this system whose entropy changes.  To calculate for the reservoirs, we need to convert the temperatures to Kelvin, so and . The entropy change of the hot reservoir is   Therefore, since , it follows that must be positive by at least this amount. so   We get the maximum work by subtracting this from :   In reality, the work output is only about 1\/3 of this amount, due to (i) design choices to get the work out faster, i.e. to get more power, (ii) friction, and (iii) imperfect combustion of the fuel.   "
