  "number": "10.4.3",
  "title": "A Two-Brick Heat Engine.",
  "body": " A Two-Brick Heat Engine   A hot brick initially at temperature is used as the source for a heat engine. An equal sized cold brick made of the same material, initially at temperature , is used to dump the waste heat. As the heat flows, the two bricks come to thermal equilibrium. Assuming the heat engine was maximally efficient, what is the final temperature?   Solution: You might think the final temperature should just be , but that would be true only if we didn't intercept any of the heat and convert it to work.  There is a lot we aren't given. We don't know the amounts or the composition of the bricks, but we know they are identical. The hotter brick is going to cool from down to some , so its entropy change will be , which is negative since . The colder brick will warm from to , so its entropy change will be . For maximum efficiency, then,   The factors divide out, and we get and so   We see that the bricks end up slightly cooler than , which makes sense since we skimmed off some of the energy as it was flowing from hot to cold.   "
