  "url": "chap_heat_engines-8.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "10.7",
  "title": "Problems",
  "body": " Problems    A ice cube at is dropped into of water at . Heat flows from the water to the ice, bringing both to equilibrium at .  Determine the number of moles of ice melted in cooling the water to .  Calculate the entropy change of the melted ice.  Calculate the entropy change of the of water in this process. Is your answer consistent with the second law?        Consider a pair of ideal solids. Solid initially has a temperature of , while solid has a temperature of . The solids are brought into thermal contact, and heat flows until the system reaches equilibrium. Determine the entropy change of each solid, and the total entropy change.      By what factor is the multiplicity increased in melting of ice into water?      A heat engine draws of heat per cycle from a hot reservoir at , and dumps of heat per cycle into a cold reservoir at . Determine the efficiency of this engine.      Consider a brick of ideal solid initially at temperature . This brick is used as the heat source for a heat engine, which is dumping heat to a room-temperature reservoir at .  Determine the entropy change of the brick as it cools to room temperature.  Determine the minimum amount of heat that must be dumped by this heat engine.  Calculate the efficiency of this best-case engine.        Consider the two-brick heat engine of Example . Calculate the maximum amount of work that could be obtained from this engine. For this problem, assume that the bricks are each an ideal solid with .      Susquehanna Valley Limousine has modified their automobile engine to provide heat for an oven to bake fresh chocolate chip cookies while you cruise Lewisburg. Not really. For each gallon of gas, of heat is dumped from the engine into the oven at a temperature of , and the remainder of the heat coming from the engine is dumped to the environment at , as described in Example . Calculate the maximum work possible from a gallon of gas for this engine. As in Example , assume that the hot reservoir has a temperature of and the heat coming from the burning gas totals .   Engine diagram for Problem        In the cycle shown below, of a monatomic ideal gas is initially at a pressure of and a temperature of . The gas is heated at constant volume to and is then expanded adiabatically until its pressure is back to . Finally, the gas is compressed at constant pressure until it is back to its original state . Find  the pressure, volume and temperature for each of the three labeled points (A, B and C),  the heat entering or leaving the system during each process, and  the efficiency of this cycle.     Cycle for Problem        For the rectangular gas cycle in Figure , assume that there is of diatomic gas.  If this cycle is to operate between two reservoirs, calculate the minimum possible value for and the maximum value for .  Compare the efficiency of this gas cycle to the maximum efficiency possible for a cycle operating between these two reservoirs.        Explain why, for any gas cycle that is clockwise on a - diagram, the area enclosed by the loop gives the net work by the gas in the cycle.      The - diagram ( Figure ) shows a complete cycle for an engine. The table below shows the thermal energy change for the first process ( ) and the work done on the engine for the third process ( ).   For problem .     Fill in numerical values for the energies (with correct signs) for every empty box in the table. Hint: fill in the easiest ones first, and there is a particular law that you should be using liberally (it rhymes with the Worst Claw of Schermodynamics ).  Determine the efficiency of this engine.          An Otto cycle for a fixed number of moles of a diatomic ideal gas is shown in Figure .   Otto cycle for Problem .     Calculate the pressure at points and .  Determine , , and for one cycle. Hint: save for last.  Calculate efficiency of this cycle.       Derive , the upper bound for in a refrigerator, by starting from the first and second laws.      Can you cool off your house by opening the door to the refrigerator? Explain why or why not.      A refrigerator is designed to keep its interior at a constant while dumping heat into a room temperature environment at . The interior of the refrigerator may be regarded as a cold reservoir, while the air around it is a hot reservoir. Now suppose a pitcher containing of room-temperature water is put into the refrigerator. We can break down what happens next into two steps: (1) the water will cool down to the temperature of the cold reservoir (fridge interior) as its heat flows into cold reservoir, and (2) the refrigerator will turn on and do work (run a compressor, circulate refrigerant, etc.) to extract this heat from the cold interior and dump it to the air outside.  We'll calculate what happens in each of these steps.  Step #1:  Calculate the change in entropy of the water as it cools from to .  Calculate the change in entropy of the cold reservoir (i.e. the inside of the refrigerator) due to this same heat transfer.  Is the second law of thermodynamics satisfied in this step? Explain briefly why or why not, using your results above.    Step #2  Now we must get rid of the extra heat in the cold reservoir by transferring it to the hot reservoir. Calculate the change in the entropy of the cold reservoir (refrigerator) as the excess heat from step #1 is removed from the refrigerator's interior.  Determine the minimum amount of work required to remove this excess heat from the cold reservoir.      "
