  "title": "Problems",
  "body": " Problems    Use dimensional analysis on the definition of the Schwarzschild radius to show that it has dimensions of length.      Calculate the Schwarzschild radius for the following objects.  A neutron star with mass .  Jupiter.        You find yourself near a black hole with the coordinate equal to three times the Schwarzschild radius.  How much does the coordinate increase if you move a distance directly away from the black hole.  How would you measure this increase in ?        You still find yourself near a black hole with the coordinate equal to three times the Schwarzschild radius.  A time of elapses on your clock. In that time interval how many seconds elapse on a clock very far away?  You move to a new location where for every that elapses on your clock, elapses on the remote clock. Determine your new coordinate in terms of the Schwarzschild radius.        Special relativity review! The Millennium Tortoise and the Millennium Hare are racing from Earth to Alpha Centauri, a distance of in the Earth frame. The Tortoise travels at a constant speed of . The Hare waits on Earth for 3 years after the Tortoise departs, and then moves at the blazing speed of to Alpha Centauri.  Show that the Tortoise and the Hare arrive at Alpha Centauri at the same time.  Calculate how much time has elapsed for the Tortoise during this trip.  Calculate how much time has elapsed for the Hare during this trip.        In the movie Interstellar the crew visits a planet that is orbiting a black hole. The gravitational time dilation is so strong that one hour spent on the planet corresponds to 7 years of time far away from the black hole. Calculate the coordinate of the planet's location in terms of the Schwarzschild radius. Express your answer as , that is, calculate the small number.      A neutron star has mass of and a circumference of . An object orbits this star in a circular orbit with circumference . How far above the surface of the neutron star is the object?      For path (b) in Figure , assume you travel at velocity for the first leg, and remain stationary, , for the rest of the trip.  Calculate the -coordinate at the junction point.  Determine the proper time for each leg and add them to get the total proper time.  Compare to the proper time of for the direct route.        Explain why gravitational lensing could cause a distant point-source of light, such as a distant galaxy, to look like a ring.      Consider a black hole with the mass of the Sun.  Determine the largest radius that this black hole could have.  Determine the minimum density that this black hole must have.      "
